## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a simple, predictable memory allocator suitable for tight RTOS constraints, with deterministic O(1) operations and minimal fragmentation.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size pool design
- Free-list management via indices
- Interrupt-safe critical sections
- Pointer arithmetic and alignment
- Stress-testing under ISR vs task context

## Code Example

```c
#define BLOCK_SIZE 64
#define BLOCK_COUNT 512
static uint8_t pool_mem[BLOCK_SIZE*BLOCK_COUNT];
static uint16_t next_index[BLOCK_COUNT];
static uint16_t free_head;

void pool_init(void) {
  for (uint16_t i = 0; i < BLOCK_COUNT-1; ++i) next_index[i] = i+1;
  next_index[BLOCK_COUNT-1] = 0xFFFF;
  free_head = 0;
}

void* pool_alloc(void) {
  if (free_head == 0xFFFF) return NULL;
  uint16_t idx = free_head;
  free_head = next_index[idx];
  return pool_mem + idx * BLOCK_SIZE;
}

void pool_free(void* ptr) {
  uintptr_t off = (uintptr_t)ptr - (uintptr_t)pool_mem;
  uint16_t idx = off / BLOCK_SIZE;
  next_index[idx] = free_head;
  free_head = idx;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to multiple block sizes?
- How would you detect and recover from pool exhaustion in real-time constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical knowledge of transactional memory in real workloads, careful fallback design, and low-latency queues.

## Key Concepts
- Intel TSX (_xbegin/_xend)
- CAS-based fallback paths
- Per-slot sequence counters to avoid ABA
- Cache-line alignment and false sharing avoidance
- Microbenchmarking for latency and throughput

## Code Example
```c
// Skeleton TSX-based enqueue
typedef struct { unsigned long long seq; unsigned long long val; } slot_t;
typedef struct { slot_t *slots; unsigned head, tail, cap; } q_t;
static inline int enqueue_htm(q_t *q, unsigned long long val) {
    unsigned pos = q->tail;
    if ((pos + 1) % q->cap == q->head) return -1; // full

    unsigned status = _xbegin();
    if (status == _XBEGIN_STARTED) {
        q->slots[pos].val = val;
        q->slots[pos].seq = pos + 1;
        q->tail = (pos + 1) % q->cap;
        _xend();
        return 0;
    }

    // Fallback: CAS-based enqueue with sequence validation
    return enqueue_cas(q, val);
}
```

## Design Considerations
- Retry limits: 3-5 TSX attempts before permanent fallback
- Alignment: 64-byte cache line boundaries
- Contention handling: exponential backoff in fallback path
- Benchmark: measure 99th percentile latency under 1 vs 16 producer/consumer pairs

